/** @mainpage Multi-dependency Example Test Suite

@section mde_overview Overview

This suite tests the
<a href="https://example.invalid/multi-dependency-example">multi-dependency-example</a>:
eight small projects in C++, Rust, Go, C# and Python wired into two dependency
cycles. In the native @b ring each stage exports a C ABI and calls the next
language, passing a value on until a hop counter runs out. In the @b web tier
three HTTP services do the same over the network. A value that enters either
cycle must come back having visited every stage.

The example ships as a corpus for review tools, with one trivial always-true
defect injected into every source file. The defect short-circuits each stage,
so a value stops after the very first one. These tests are written against the
@e correct behaviour, so on the as-shipped example they fail with stable
known-issue verdicts recorded in the TRC database, and pass once the defects
are fixed.

@section mde_groups Groups

- @ref mde-ring     - run cpp-demo / rust-demo / the Python demo and check every
                      stage line against a model of the five formulas.
- @ref mde-web      - start the three HTTP services and walk the web cycle:
                      cycle, latency, contract, robustness.
- @ref mde-webdeps  - hypotheses H1-H5 about gateway-rs, service-go and
                      service-cs, the per-language builds, the built binaries
                      and one smoke test per language.
- @ref mde-graph    - check that the libraries are linked into the cycle the
                      way the build intends (no running code involved).
- @ref mde-package  - runtime dependencies mapped to packages (tsf-package).
- @ref mde-devtool  - rebuild parts of the example on the agent (tsf-devtool).
- @ref mde-fs       - permissions, secrets and integrity of the tree (tsf-fs).
- @ref mde-memmap   - ELF images and live mappings (tsf-memorymap).
- @ref mde-binary   - hardening of every binary (tsf-cybersec).
- @ref mde-exposure - listeners, reachability, egress, traffic (tsf-cybersec).
- @ref mde-http     - each service as an HTTP server (tsf-http).
- @ref mde-container - the ring inside other images (tsf-container).
- @ref mde-fuzz     - the ring's C ABI under a fuzzer (tsf-fuzz).
- @ref mde-browser  - the web tier in a real browser (tsf-playwright).

@section mde_verdicts Reading the results

Each test fills one finding report. Where the test settles a hypothesis, the
verdict starts with its id and says CONFIRMED, DISPROVED, PASS or FAIL with
what was measured. A service that does not start fails with "not started" and
settles nothing. A SKIP names the tool, hardware or kernel feature that is
missing. Known failures are listed in @c conf/known-issues.json and recorded in
@c conf/trc.xml.

@section mde_running Running

Run @c scripts/run.sh. It builds a native private copy of the example for the
agent's host (see @c scripts/build-dut.sh), unless @c TE_TS_MDE_BUILD=0.
@c TE_TS_MDE_SRC points the suite at another example tree. Group prologues
install the tools their tests need on the agent and fail with the cause when
an install does not work. Reports are written to @c reports/ (start with
@c index.html); @c TE_TS_MDE_REPORTS=0 skips them.

*/
